{
  "packet_id": "85a13c13187ad36b",
  "dimensions": {
    "problem_framing": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 2,
      "event_ids": ["A1"],
      "reason": "A1 separates the objective (whether evidence justifies investment) from the suggested solution. It lists real alternatives, including no expansion, a route-to-market change and a different professional customer group. It does not flag the non-obvious premise that consumer-operated shared laundry is already part of the existing professional offering (epro-consumer-operated, epro-selfservice-formats), so it is not 'beyond' it. It also misses the channel-absence constraint implied by epro-reviewed-speedqueen-small. The €20,000 and 12-week constraints do not appear in the case dossier and are unexplained."
    },
    "causal_mechanism": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 1,
      "event_ids": ["A5", "A6"],
      "reason": "The best theory, T1 (A5), names a benefit (lower operational cost, uptime) but confuses who buys. Residents and students in multi-family buildings do not usually purchase the shared machines; building operators do. 'Residents ... benefit from reduced labor costs' is incoherent as a behaviour-change chain. Value capture through premium machines and service contracts is asserted, not traced from a coherent buyer decision."
    },
    "distinct_theories": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 1,
      "event_ids": ["A5", "A6"],
      "reason": "T1 and T2 share the same product, customer, channel and settings, and even an identical prediction (higher adoption in multi-family than single-family). They differ only in the stated motive, cost versus convenience. They are near-rewordings of one adoption idea. Neither is a rival such as household premium durability, retail/wholesale compact machines (epro-gen7000-retail-wholesale) or no viable consumer demand."
    },
    "assumptions_predictions": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 2,
      "event_ids": ["A5", "A6"],
      "reason": "The predictions are observable, for example survey choices and willingness to pay with a downtime warranty. However, they are stated-preference survey outcomes, so T1 would not be in serious trouble if they failed. The 10% thresholds are unexplained assumptions. The shared adoption prediction does not discriminate between the theories."
    },
    "evidence_judgement": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 1,
      "event_ids": ["A7", "A8", "A13"],
      "reason": "A7 labels epro-alliance-residential-segment an 'independent_study'. It is a verified observation of another company's statement about 'our machines' and a select residential home segment. A7 then applies it to self-service shared spaces, which the quote does not cover. A8 cites a company claim describing capabilities as support for T2. The 'does not show' limits are partly sensible, but there is no attention to source type, place, fit with the customer group, or the ignored sources (epro-reviewed-speedqueen-small, epro-uba-replace-working). A13 mischaracterises the evidence base as 'a single simulated result ... and a company claim'."
    },
    "seeking_challenge": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 1,
      "event_ids": ["A9"],
      "reason": "A9 searches against T1 and lists 'epro-which-no-premium', a source absent from the dossier, as potentially contrary. It never reports or weighs what was found, and no later event engages it. This amounts to looking without reporting fairly."
    },
    "experiment_design": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 1,
      "event_ids": ["A10"],
      "reason": "Comparing a cost frame with a convenience frame could in principle separate T1 from T2. The design has several defects. It samples residents and students, who are not the machine buyers. It mixes a 'percentage points' rule with average willingness to pay. The decision rule is truncated ('consider a'). It never tests the go/no-go question shared by both theories. Its decision relevance and validity are therefore weak."
    },
    "revision": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 1,
      "event_ids": ["A12", "A13"],
      "reason": "A12 states that SYN-EPRO-UPDATE 'does not bear on T1'. Yet the durability and repairability framing, which aligns with T1's long-term reliability and uptime value, drew 32 reservations versus 72 for the performance-and-price framing. That bears at least on T1's premium-for-reliability assumption, even allowing that household buyers are a different population. A13 then lists as a change condition 'if the simulated result were to show a meaningful difference', when the result already showed one in the direction against T1. Retention may have been defensible on population-fit grounds, but the stated reasoning misreads the update."
    },
    "recommendation": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 1,
      "event_ids": ["A11", "A13"],
      "reason": "A13 recommends 'Invest' while its own next action is a validating survey. This conflicts with the A1 constraint that any recommendation rest on observable demand. The support rests on a misapplied quote. One reversal condition is illogical: 'strong consumer demand ... we would reconsider the investment'. The recommendation also targets a format the company already offers professionally. It states a position without adequate support."
    }
  },
  "red_flags": [
    {
      "kind": "unsupported_inference",
      "event_ids": ["A7", "A11", "A13"],
      "reason": "A quote about a competitor's machines for a select residential home segment is used as support for premium self-service machines in shared buildings. That support then underpins an 'Invest' recommendation."
    },
    {
      "kind": "unsupported_inference",
      "event_ids": ["A8"],
      "reason": "A company capability claim (epro-consumer-operated) is treated as support for a demand theory, which amounts to a marketing claim used as evidence of demand."
    },
    {
      "kind": "unsupported_inference",
      "event_ids": ["A5"],
      "reason": "The mechanism has residents buying shared-building machines and benefiting from 'reduced labor costs', which is an incoherent buyer chain."
    },
    {
      "kind": "unsound_revision",
      "event_ids": ["A12", "A13"],
      "reason": "The update dismisses a relevant simulated result as irrelevant to T1, and then treats the already-observed large difference as hypothetical."
    },
    {
      "kind": "other",
      "event_ids": ["A9"],
      "reason": "The challenge search cites 'epro-which-no-premium', which does not exist in the dossier. Its content is never reported or weighed, so potential counter-evidence was ignored."
    },
    {
      "kind": "other",
      "event_ids": ["A10"],
      "reason": "The test samples non-buyers, mixes the units of the decision rule, and has a truncated decision rule."
    },
    {
      "kind": "other",
      "event_ids": ["A1"],
      "reason": "The budget and timeline constraints (€20,000, 12 weeks) do not appear in the case dossier and are not explained."
    }
  ],
  "suitable_for_training": false,
  "screen_reason": "The episode is complete, but it contains several critical errors. A source is misclassified and misapplied as support (A7). A company claim is used as evidence of demand (A8). The update misreads a relevant simulated result (A12, A13). Challenge evidence from an off-dossier source is ignored (A9). The recommendation to invest is unsupported (A13). Any one of these rejects the trajectory."
}

{
  "packet_id": "9bc4db73219694ea",
  "dimensions": {
    "problem_framing": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 2,
      "event_ids": ["A1"],
      "reason": "A1 separates the objective (whether evidence justifies investment) from the suggested solution. It lists real alternatives: no expansion, a route-to-market change, and a different professional customer group. It also sets constraints such as 'based on observable demand'. It does not question the premise or define what 'specific use case' means. The company name is garbled ('Electron’x'). It does not reach rung 3."
